You are an adversarial reviewer of scientific figures for a research proposal. The attached PNGs are rendered at 2x from SVG and will be displayed about 850 px wide. Try hard to find reasons each figure should not ship. For EACH image, by file order, report: (a) overlapping or colliding text, (b) lines or arrows that cross text or other arrows confusingly, (c) arrows whose direction or endpoints are ambiguous, (d) text too small to read at display size, (e) excess text that belongs in a caption, (f) the one main point and whether a reader gets it in 5 seconds, (g) concrete fixes with coordinates or element names. Then give a verdict per figure: ship / fix / redraw. Focus: Proposal 05, 'What makes an agent harness easier to improve?'. Round 2 after fixes; judge each figure on its own merits. Points, in file order: (1) same-outputs-different-futures: two harnesses with identical outputs could improve at different rates under one optimizer and budget; the gap is a hypothesis and the curves are illustrative. (2) pipeline-two-forms: the StaBLE Y-Balance score computed from the same cached keypoints as one function and as typed stages with configured thresholds, warning-only checks and a per-stage trace the optimizer reads; task scores are the same. (3) bpt-equivalence-gate: a seed harness is rewritten with one level of each of five factors (M, O, P, T, N), matched to one token count, and admitted only if differential tests find the same task scores and tool calls. (4) single-edit-profile: thirty one-step edits from the same parent, in three sessions of ten, are sorted into invalid, valid with gain under 1%, and valid with selection error down at least 1%, then summarized as the profile (v, s, g, r); pattern illustrative. (5) refactor-first-break-even: in an illustrative accept-if-better model, a refactor-first arm spends a fixed cost with no gain, then overtakes search alone at the break-even budget B*. (6) parent-selection-at-birth: hypothesis figure; for a newborn node evaluated once with no descendants, DGM sees its current dev score, HGM's clade estimate rests on one count (a broad density), and a structural prior from code features gives a concentrated density on the same clade-success scale. (7) participant-splits: 171 participants split by person into 85 development (scores, traces, normalization bounds), 43 selection (edit success, final candidate) and 43 sealed test people scored once per loop. (8) two-tier-design: tier 1 is an eight-cell resolution IV half fraction (I = MOPT) crossed with 20 bases and 3 proposers at 30 edits each; tier 2 gives 96 configurations a profile and a clade estimate at matched cost plus ten loops each, and compares both predictors with sealed-test gain within base.. Do not run commands or modify files.

